<!-- a first draft of this preview was written by Siddhi Wakdikar after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

  <setup>
    <function name="p" variable="t">0.25*e^(-0.25t)</function>
    <function name="P" variable="t">-e^(-0.25t)</function>
    <number name="ans1">$$P(10)-$$P(5)</number>
    <number name="ans2">$$P(20)-$$P(10)</number>
    <number name="ans3">$$P(5)-$$P(0)</number>
    <number name="ans4">$$P(10)-$$P(0)</number>
    <number name="ans5">$$P(15)-$$P(0)</number>
    <number name="ans6">$$P(20)-$$P(0)</number>
  </setup>

  <p>
    A company with a large customer base has a call center that receives thousands of calls a day.
    After studying the data that represents how long callers wait for assistance,
    they find that the function
    <m>p(t) = 0.25e^{-0.25t}</m> models the time customers wait in the following way:
    the fraction of customers who waited at least <m>a</m> minutes but not more than <m>b</m> minutes is given by
    <me>
      \int_a^b p(t) \, dt.
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Use this information to answer the following questions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p>
          Determine the fraction of callers who wait at least <m>5</m> minutes but less than <m>10</m> minutes.  Enter your result as a single number in decimal form, accurate to within <m>0.001</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fraction waiting between <m>5</m> and <m>10</m> minutes: 
          <mathInput name = "a1"><shortDescription>waiting 5 to 10 minutes</shortDescription></mathInput>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$a1=$ans1
          </when></award></answer>
        </p>
        
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Determine the fraction of callers who wait at least <m>10</m> minutes but at most <m>20</m> minutes. Enter your result as a single number in decimal form, accurate to within <m>0.001</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fraction waiting between <m>10</m> and <m>20</m> minutes: 
          <mathInput name = "a2"><shortDescription>waiting 10 to 20 minutes</shortDescription></mathInput>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$a2=$ans2
          </when></award></answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Next, let's study the fraction who wait <em>up to</em> (that is, <em>at most</em>) a certain number of minutes.  For each of the following prompts, again enter a single decimal value, accurate to within <m>0.001</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <label>The fraction waiting at most <m>5</m> minutes is:</label>
            $ans3
          </answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <label>The fraction waiting at most <m>10</m> minutes is:</label>
            $ans4
          </answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <label>The fraction waiting at most <m>15</m> minutes is:</label>
            $ans5
          </answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer symbolicEquality allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <label>The fraction waiting at most <m>20</m> minutes is:</label>
            $ans6
          </answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>F(b)</m> represent the fraction of callers who wait at most <m>b</m> minutes.
          Fill in the appropriate blanks below to state a formula for <m>F(b)</m> that involves a definite integral.
        </p>
              
          <p>
            <answer name="lower"><label>lower limit of integration: </label>0</answer>
          </p>
          <p>
            <answer name="upper"><label>upper limit of integration: </label>b</answer>
          </p>
  
          <p>
            Integral expression for <m>F(b)</m>, the fraction of people who wait at most <m>b</m> minutes: 
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>F(b) = \displaystyle\int_{$lower}^{$upper}</m><mathInput name="int1" minWidth="100" ><shortDescription>integrand</shortDescription></mathInput><m>dt \, </m><answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$int1 = $p</when></award></answer>
          </p>        
          <p>
            Now apply the First FTC to evaluate the integral above to find a different, equivalent formula for <m>F(b)</m> that does not involve a definite integral. 
          </p>
          <p>  
            <m>F(b) = </m>
            <mathInput minWidth="125" name = "a8">
              <shortDescription>result of FTC</shortDescription>
            </mathInput>
              <answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton>
              <award>
                <when>
                  $a8=-e^(-b/4)+1
                </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          What is the value of <m>\displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} F(b)</m>?
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} F(b) = </m>
          <mathInput name = "a9">
            <shortDescription>limit value</shortDescription>
          </mathInput>
          <answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton name="a9ANS" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <award>
              <when>
                $a9=1
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          Complete the following sentence to explain the meaning of <m>\displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} F(b)</m>.
        </p>
        <setup>
          <conditionalContent name='nextStep' condition="$a9ANS.creditAchieved = 1"> 
            <math name="math_a9" extend="$a9.value"/>
          </conditionalContent>
        </setup>
        <p>
          Our work in (c) shows that as the end of the wait time, <m>b</m>, increases, the fraction of customers who have waited for <m>b</m> minutes <choiceInput inline name="c1">
            <shortDescription>increases, decreases, stays same</shortDescription>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice>increases</choice>
            <choice>decreases</choice>
            <choice>stays the same</choice>
          </choiceInput>, and since <m>\displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} F(b) = </m><mathInput minWidth="25" name="a10" prefill="$nextStep.math_a9"><shortDescription>limit value from prior question, input automatically</shortDescription></mathInput>, this means that <choiceInput inline name="c2">
            <shortDescription>possible explanations</shortDescription>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice>1 person didn't have to wait at all</choice>
            <choice>eventually every call is answered</choice>
            <choice>all calls are answered within 1 minute</choice>
          </choiceInput>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer><award><when>$c1.selectedIndices=2 and $c2.selectedIndices=3</when></award></answer>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>